ite #32; 2602.70 dig white #32'). Earlier attempts to enforce full multi-contact sequences failed (gen_04/cand_2, gen_05/cand_3), whereas appending to the dig rule's 'never log' clause worked strongly in gen_07/cand_1. Appending 'or a second contact after a teammate's touch' to the negative clause of the dig rule will prune invented second-contact digs while preserving positive dig recall.", "prediction": "Predicted digs drop from 74 to ~66 while matched digs remain at 64 and missed events stay near 40, reducing invented events by ~8 and raising OVERALL F1 by +0.017.", "lessons": {"after_gen": 12, "text": "- Filtering out routine sets unless they directly precede a point-winning kill is the single largest lever, raising overall F1 from 0.621 to 0.701 (gen_01/cand_0, +0.080) by cutting invented sets.\n- Enforcing mutual exclusivity between 'attack' and 'kill' while restricting 'block' to rally-ending stuff blocks works strongly, lifting F1 from 0.701 to 0.735 (gen_02/cand_0, +0.034) and cutting invented events from 230 to 150.\n- Suppressing defensive touches after a point-winning kill and requiring digs to successfully keep an attack in play raises F1 from 0.735 to 0.759 (gen_03/cand_0, +0.024) by reducing invented events from 150 to 117.\n- Excluding free-ball passes/bumps from both attacks and digs in pairs works best, pushing F1 from 0.759 to 0.780 (gen_07/cand_1, +0.021) and dropping invented events from 117 to 82, whereas filtering free-ball attacks alone peaked at 0.768 (gen_06/cand_0).\n- Narrowing positive action definitions (e.g., changing \"opponent's attack\" to \"opponent's spike\" in dig or block rules) trades fewer inventions for severe recall loss, spiking missed events from 33 to 54 in gen_05/cand_0 (F1 0.773) and from 40 to 48\u201356 in gen_08/cand_3 and gen_09/cand_1.\n- Adding negative exclusions while keeping positive definitions verbatim preserves recall far better than rewriting positive clauses, as shown by gen_07/cand_1 (40 missed, 0.780 F1) vs gen_05/cand_0 (54 missed, 0.773 F1) and gen_11/cand_2 (45 missed, 0.783 single-run F1) vs gen_09/cand_2 (59 missed, 0.776 F1).\n- Providing valid jersey number rosters or team shirt colors consistently trades a small gain in number F1 for worse overall action detection and recall across seven attempts (gen_01/cand_2, gen_02/cand_3, gen_03/cand_3, gen_04/cand_0, gen_07/cand_2, gen_11/cand_0, gen_12/cand_3), e.g., gen_03/cand_3 raised number F1 by +0.043 but dropped overall F1 to 0.750.\n- Removing `or null` or instructing the model to inspect nearby clear frames to read blurred jersey numbers does not work, dropping F1 from 0.759 to 0.751 (gen_06/cand_3, -0.008) and from 0.780 to 0.769 (gen_10/cand_2, -0.011) while raising missed events to 59.\n- Attempting to fix early serve timestamps via prompt instructions (anchoring `t` to hand strike, net crossing, or banning pre-serve tosses/duplicate serves) failed in all six attempts (gen_01/cand_3, gen_02/cand_2, gen_03/cand_2, gen_04/cand_1, gen_08/cand_2, gen_10/cand_1), lowering F1 by -0.007 to -0.047.\n- Adding rules to stop logging dead-ball touches after a rally ends or on deflected kills does not work in isolation, increasing invented events from 117 to 138 in gen_05/cand_1 (F1 0.756) and to 135 in gen_06/cand_2 (F1 0.753).\n- Enforcing strict multi-contact possession sequences or forbidding consecutive same-team touches degrades performance, dropping F1 to 0.761 (gen_04/cand_2), 0.745 (gen_05/cand_3), and 0.763 (gen_07/cand_3).\n- Tightening the set rule with cross-class references (e.g., \"never before a non-scoring attack\", \"teammate's kill\", or \"never log a ball sent over the net as a set\") consistently hurts recall, raising missed events from 40 to 50\u201355 and dropping F1 to 0.757\u20130.772 (gen_08/cand_0, gen_10/cand_3, gen_12/cand_1).\n- Clarifying attack errors vs kills\u2014either in the logging rules or in the schema field descriptions (`point`/`error`)\u2014does not improve F1, scoring 0.759 (gen_08/cand_1), 0.773 (gen_09/cand_0), and 0.764 (gen_10/cand_0) vs the 0.780 baseline.\n- Open question: Gen_11/cand_2 achieved a 0.783 single-run F1 (+0.003 over gen_07/cand_1) by appending \"; never log a first-contact dig or a ball sent over the net as a set\", warranting a multi-run confirmation test to verify if this gain holds above noise.\n- Open question: Since prompt engineering could not fix systematic ~1.2s early serve timestamps or out-of-roster jersey numbers without hurting action recall, can deterministic post-processing (shifting serve `t` by +1.0s and filtering/mapping invalid jersey numbers) capture those gains safely?\n"}, "parent_tiers": {"overall": 0.7878, "time": 0.8739, "action": 0.834, "team": 0.8298, "number": 0.6134}, "parent_counts": {"ok": 395, "wrong_team": 2, "wrong_action": 18, "missed": 40, "invented": 82}, "parent_per_type": {"serve": {"real": 97, "predicted": 104, "matched": 82, "f1": 0.816}, "receive": {"real": 86, "predicted": 86, "matched": 80, "f1": 0.93}, "dig": {"real": 70, "predicted": 74, "matched": 64, "f1": 0.889}, "set": {"real": 41, "predicted": 58, "matched": 36, "f1": 0.727}, "attack": {"real": 110, "predicted": 116, "matched": 94, "f1": 0.832}, "kill": {"real": 44, "predicted": 49, "matched": 39, "f1": 0.839}, "block": {"real": 7, "predicted": 10, "matched": 2, "f1": 0.235}}, "input": "evo2data/input_13.txt"}